From f62b97d4072f7743aacf77ca74878f37f7373976 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 20:56:34 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 29/35] Delay: time change without pitch bend (Fade), clean
 first repeat and tail, 4 s buffers

- New per-delay setting "Time change" (Delay menu, after Type): Fade (default)
  or Tape. In Fade, a new delay time starts in a fresh buffer at its own speed;
  what goes into the delay crossfades into it over 1024 samples, while the old
  buffer plays out what it holds at its own speed, so no repeat changes pitch
  (measured within 0.01 cent) and nothing clicks. A new time is taken after
  31 ms without change or 125 ms of turning, one change at a time, within a
  0.05 % tolerance for tempo drift. Tape is the previous behaviour (pitch glide),
  bit-exact apart from the fixes below. Saved as delay attribute timeChange
  (0 fade, 1 tape); files without it load as Fade.
- First repeat: the first buffer of a delay was only read after its content had
  come round, at the end of that block, losing up to 128 samples of the first
  repeat (a click on every first echo after a pause). It now takes over while
  the read position is in a cleared stretch (1024 samples cleared).
- End of the repeats: the last time round before the delay gives up fades out
  instead of cutting off 42 to 60 dB down; comes back if sound comes in.
- Largest buffer 4 s (was 2 s): 2 to 4 s delays no longer resample (images of a
  15 kHz tone only 7 dB down at 2.5 s before).
- A tape-mode buffer made right after a fade glides from the rate the primary
  buffer really ran at (no step when it takes over).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Eb214cM1zi1k5Gpcn9RLKh
